# `milp::Status`

The verdict of a mixed-integer solve.

`Optimal` means an incumbent was proven optimal within `gap_tolerance`. `Infeasible` means the relaxation is infeasible or every node was pruned without an incumbent, so no integer solution exists. `Unbounded` is reported when the relaxation is unbounded (integrality is not examined further). `NodeLimit` means `max_nodes` was reached; `x` holds the incumbent if one was found and `best_bound` the proven bound.

## Fields

### `Optimal`

`enumerator`, default `0`.

The incumbent is proven optimal within `gap_tolerance`.

### `Infeasible`

`enumerator`, default `1`.

No integer-feasible solution exists.

### `Unbounded`

`enumerator`, default `2`.

The relaxation is unbounded.

### `NodeLimit`

`enumerator`, default `3`.

`max_nodes` was reached before the search finished.

## Guidance

Only `Optimal` carries a proof of optimality.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

Optimal and infeasible verdicts.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace milp = pluss::subsp::math::milp;
namespace linprog = pluss::subsp::math::linprog;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  // maximise 10a + 13b + 7c + 8d   subject to   3a + 4b + 2c + 3d <= 7,  each item taken or not
  milp::Problem problem;
  problem.lp.c = pluss::series::make({-10.0, -13.0, -7.0, -8.0});
  problem.lp.a_ub = pluss::series::make({{3.0, 4.0, 2.0, 3.0}});
  problem.lp.b_ub = pluss::series::make({7.0});
  problem.lp.lower = pluss::series::make({0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0});
  problem.lp.upper = pluss::series::make({1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0});
  problem.integer = pluss::series::make({1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0});

  milp::Problem none;
  none.lp.c = pluss::series::make({1.0});
  none.lp.lower = pluss::series::make({0.2});
  none.lp.upper = pluss::series::make({0.8});
  none.integer = pluss::series::make({1.0});
  out.writeln("knapsack :", milp::to_string(milp::solve(problem).status));
  out.writeln("no integer:", milp::to_string(milp::solve(none).status));
}
```

### Example 2

`Unbounded`. The relaxation of `min −y` with `y ≥ 0` free to grow, next to a bounded integer `x`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <limits>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace milp = pluss::subsp::math::milp;
namespace linprog = pluss::subsp::math::linprog;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  milp::Problem problem;
  problem.lp.c = pluss::series::make({1.0, -1.0});
  problem.lp.lower = pluss::series::make({0.0, 0.0});
  problem.lp.upper = pluss::series::make({3.0, std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity()});
  problem.integer = pluss::series::make({1.0, 0.0});
  out.writeln("status =", milp::to_string(milp::solve(problem).status));
}
```

### Example 3

`NodeLimit`. A cut-off search keeps the bound.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace milp = pluss::subsp::math::milp;
namespace linprog = pluss::subsp::math::linprog;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  // maximise 10a + 13b + 7c + 8d   subject to   3a + 4b + 2c + 3d <= 7,  each item taken or not
  milp::Problem problem;
  problem.lp.c = pluss::series::make({-10.0, -13.0, -7.0, -8.0});
  problem.lp.a_ub = pluss::series::make({{3.0, 4.0, 2.0, 3.0}});
  problem.lp.b_ub = pluss::series::make({7.0});
  problem.lp.lower = pluss::series::make({0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0});
  problem.lp.upper = pluss::series::make({1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0});
  problem.integer = pluss::series::make({1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0});

  milp::Options options;
  options.max_nodes = 1;
  const milp::Result result = milp::solve(problem, options);
  out.writeln("status =", milp::to_string(result.status), ", best bound =", result.best_bound);
}
```

